Baselines: vs. Compile and vs. Eager L40:     3. cite30†6.3 Fallback Analysis L41:     4. cite31†6.4 The Multi-Kernel Composition Gap L42:     5. cite32†6.5 L3 Architecture Analysis and Ablation Study L43:   8. cite33†7 Conclusion L44:   9. cite34†References L45:   10. cite35†A Agent Prompt Templates L46:     1. cite36†A.1 Planner Agent L47:     2. cite37†A.2 Executor Agent L48:     3. cite38†A.3 Summarizer Agent L49:     4. cite39†A.4 Profiler Agent L50:     5. cite40†A.5 Codegen (Synthesis) Agent L51:     6. cite41†A.6 Fusion Agent L52:   11. cite42†B Verification Cascade Details L53:     1. cite43†Float64 fallback (Gate 3, Tier 2). L54:     2. cite44†Full discussion: search-time selection vs. final verification. L55:     3. cite45†Weight extraction (Gate 3). L56:   12. cite46†C Shipped Optimization Guidelines L57:   13. cite47†D Ablation Results L58:     1. cite48†Sampled kernels. L59:     2. cite49†Observations. L60:   14. cite50†E NCU Profiling Case Studies L61:     1. cite51†Why sub-kernel and E2E speedups diverge. L62:     2. cite52†L1-007: Register pressure reduction. L63:     3. cite53†L1-012: Algorithmic rewrite. L64:     4. cite54†L2-018: Operator fusion with algebraic simplification. L65:   15. cite55†F Complete KernelBench Results L66:     1. cite56†F.1 Fallback Analysis L67:     2. cite57†F.2 Multi-Kernel Composition Gap: Full Case Listing L68:     3. cite58†F.3 L3 Architecture Breakdown L69:   16. cite59†G Generated Kernel Examples L70:     1. cite60†G.1 L1 Kernel 012: Diagonal Matrix Multiply (88.63$\\times$) L71:       1. cite61†Before (Inductor via cuBLAS). L72:       2. cite62†After (KernelOPT synthesized Triton kernel). L73:       3. cite63†Optimization pass. L74:     2. cite64†G.2 L2 Kernel 018: Algebraic Simplification (33.11$\\times$) L75:     3. cite65†G.3 L1 Kernel 007: Matmul Autotune Optimization (2.89$\\times$) L76:   17. cite66†H Tutorial Kernels L77:   18. cite67†I Gate 3 Correctness Verification: $\\rho$ Distribution Analysis L78:     1. cite68†I.1 Quantitative Summary (rejected candidates only) L79:     2. cite69†I.2 Key Observations L80:   19. cite70†J Hardware and Software Configuration L81: cite71†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org L82: \nL83: arXiv:2609.30059v2 [cs.DC] 06 Oct 2026\nL84: # KernelOPT: Dispatch-Aware Agentic Search\nL85: for GPU Kernel Optimization\nL86: Aheli Poddar ^{†}^{†}thanks: Equal contribution (joint first authors).^{†}^{†}thanks: Corresponding author.^{†}^{†}thanks: The first four authors completed this work during an internship with the PyTorch team at Red Hat.\nL87: Affiliation: Red Hat Email: ahpoddar@redhat.com    Sanskar Prasad^{1}^{1}footnotemark: 1 ^{3}^{3}footnotemark: 3 Affiliation: Red Hat Email: sanspras@redhat.com    Arindam Samanta^{3}^{3}footnotemark: 3 Affiliation: Red Hat Email: arsamant@redhat.com    Subha Chakraborty^{3}^{3}footnotemark: 3 Affiliation: Red Hat Email: subhchak@redhat.com    Vishal Goyal Affiliation: Red Hat Email: visgoyal@redhat.com    Rohit Singh Rathaur Affiliation: Red Hat Email: rrathaur@redhat.com\nL88: ###### Abstract\nL89: Deep learning inference and training performance depends critically on GPU kernel efficiency. Modern compilers such as PyTorch Inductor automatically generate GPU kernels from high-level model code, but frequently underperform expert-written implementations by wide margins.\nL90: Recent LLM-assisted kernel optimizers can close this gap for standalone kernels, yet treat compiled models as black boxes, generally optimizing individual standalone kernels without respecting the compiler’s structural decisions or verifying the model end-to-end. We present KernelOPT, a multi-agent system that treats compiled models as structured artifacts. It preserves vendor library calls (cuBLAS, cuDNN) and exclusively targets generated Triton sub-kernels using five profiling-guided LLM agents.\nL91: A four-gate verification cascade applies static validation, multi-seed correctness checking, model-level float64-fallback verification, and performance gating ($\\gamma{=}1.03$) to filter candidates and verify the re-stitched model end-to-end. When candidates fail verification, the system preserves the compiler baseline. The system accepts PyTorch nn.Module s, standalone Triton kernels, and Helion kernels.\nL92: Evaluated on 250 KernelBench problems (100 Level 1, 100 Level 2 and 50 Level 3) on NVIDIA H200, KernelOPT achieves geometric mean speedups over torch.compile of 1.40$\\times$ (L1), 1.15$\\times$ (L2), and 1.07$\\times$ (L3) across all kernels, including fallback cases.\nL93: Optimized-only geomeans (excluding cases where verification gates preserve the compiler baseline) are substantially higher: 2.54$\\times$ (L1: 36/100), 1.84$\\times$ (L2: 23/100), and 1.37$\\times$ (L3: 11/50), reflecting where the optimizer achieves meaningful leverage.\nL94: ## 1 Introduction\nL95: Efficient GPU kernels are the foundation of performant deep learning. PyTorch Inductor (cite72†Ansel et al., 2024 ), the default torch.compile backend, delivers a 2.27$\\times$ geometric mean inference speedup over eager mode by lowering operations to Triton (cite73†Tillet et al., 2019 ). However, the generated code leaves substantial performance on the table.\nL96: Inductor’s autotuning explores a limited tile-size configuration space by default, its fusion heuristics are conservative, and its cross-operation rewrites are limited to a fixed set of manually specified pattern matches rather than a general algebraic simplification framework.\nL97: Expert-written kernels such as FlashAttention (cite74†Dao et al., 2022 ; cite75†Dao, 2024 ; cite76†Shah et al., 2024 ) demonstrate that near-order-of-magnitude speedups are achievable on the same hardware, but require extensive engineering effort (cite77†Zhang et al., 2026 ).\nL98: Recent work has applied LLM agents to automate this process. KernelAgent (cite78†Wang et al., 2025 ) translates PyTorch models to Triton kernels via multi-agent code-to-code generation with NCU-guided optimization. AccelOpt (cite77†Zhang et al., 2026 ) introduces an iterative planner–executor–summarizer agent loop with optimization memory for NKI kernels on AWS Trainium. K-Search (cite79†Cao et al., 2026 ) formulates kernel generation as planning over a co-evolving LLM world model with tree-structured search.\nL99: These systems share a critical limitation: while some support multi-kernel decomposition, none provides end-to-end verification of the re-stitched model against the original PyTorch model with weight-extracted correctness and performance gating.\nL100: A key observation motivates our approach: compiled models are structured artifacts where optimization effort should match what is actually optimizable. A compiled PyTorch model delegates specific operation types to vendor libraries – cuBLAS (cite80†NVIDIA Corporation, 2024a ) for GEMM, cuDNN (cite81†Chetlur et al., 2014 ) for convolution – while Triton handles the rest.\nL101: Removing dispatch preservation drops L2 optimization success to $0\\%$ (Table cite82†5 ), and $75\\%$ of our fallbacks are library-dominated (Table cite83†1 , Panel D): attempting to replace already-near-optimal vendor calls introduces more failures than gains. KernelOPT instead respects the compiler’s dispatch decisions, concentrates search on Triton sub-kernels, and verifies results at the model level rather than in isolation.\nL102: This formulation yields three main contributions: (1) Compiled-model aware kernel optimization: decomposing compiled PyTorch models while respecting cuBLAS/cuDNN dispatch, selectively optimizing Triton sub-kernels, and verifying the re-stitched result at the model level (also accepting standalone Triton and Helion (cite84†Ansel and others, 2025 ) kernels).\nL103: (2) Four-gate verification pipeline: static validation, multi-seed correctness, model-level float64-fallback checking, and performance gating ($\\gamma{=}1.03$) to filter candidates and prevent regressions (§cite19†4.4 ). (3) Profiling-guided multi-agent search: five agents (four LLM-driven, one deterministic) in a LangGraph (cite85†LangChain Inc., 2024 ) system using NCU (cite86†NVIDIA Corporation, 2024b ) profiling, with iterative in-conversation feedback and diversity enforcement against search stagnation.\nL104: The complete codebase is available at cite87†https://github.com/TorchedHat/KernelOPT†github.com .\nL105: ## 2 Related Work\nL106: #### Compiler-based and manual optimization.\nL107: Halide (cite88†Ragan-Kelley et al., 2013 ) and TVM with Ansor (cite89†Chen et al., 2018 ; cite90†Zheng et al., 2020 ) separate algorithm from schedule to enable large search spaces. Triton uses block-level programming with automatic compiler scheduling; PyTorch Inductor (cite72†Ansel et al., 2024 ) integrates Triton codegen into torch.compile but constrains autotuning to fixed tile sizes and fusion patterns.\nL108: ThunderKittens (cite91†Spector et al., 2024 ) provides tile-level CUDA abstractions for hand-writing high-performance attention and SSM kernels. Mirage (cite92†Wu et al., 2025 ) applies multi-level superoptimization with formal equivalence verification, representing the state of the art in non-LLM kernel synthesis.\nL109: #### LLM-driven kernel optimization.\nL110: KernelAgent (cite78†Wang et al., 2025 ) combines multi-agent generation with NCU profiling for Triton kernel synthesis. We replicated it on KernelBench Level 1 (identical H200 hardware and Claude Sonnet 4.6 backend): KernelAgent reaches $0.966\\times$ geomean vs. torch.compile (71/100 working, 22/71 regressed), vs. KernelOPT’s $1.649\\times$ (89/100 working); on the 68 problems both optimize, KernelOPT wins 40/68 head-to-head vs. KernelAgent’s 14/68 ($1.858\\times$ vs. $0.943\\times$ geomean).\nL111: The gap reflects KernelOPT’s four-gate verification and model-level re-stitching, which KernelAgent lacks.\nL112: AccelOpt (cite77†Zhang et al., 2026 ) introduces the iterative planner–executor–summarizer architecture with beam search and optimization memory for NKI kernels on AWS Trainium; we adapt this iterative framework to NVIDIA GPUs and extend it with Inductor-aware synthesis, model-level verification, and guided fallback. CudaForge (cite93†Zhang et al., 2025 ) uses a two-agent Judge–Coder system. K-Search (cite79†Cao et al., 2026 ) uses LLM-guided tree search with a co-evolving world model.\nL113: Astra (cite94†Wei and others, 2025 ) employs a multi-agent architecture with iterative profiling for CUDA kernel optimization but targets existing production kernels rather than compiled-model synthesis. OptiML (cite95†Bhattacharjee et al., 2026 ) combines program synthesis with MCTS-guided refinement for CUDA kernels. AutoKernel (cite96†Jaber and Jaber, 2026 ) applies an iterative keep/revert loop with model-level profiling and a five-stage correctness harness.\nL114: Among these, AutoKernel provides model-level profiling and end-to-end correctness verification; however, it does not use NCU hardware profiling to guide optimization directions. Unlike these existing methods; KernelOPT combines Inductor-aware compiled-model decomposition, NCU guided multi-agent planning, end-to-end model-level verification with float64 fallback, and compiler-baseline fallback when no candidate passes all four gates.\nL115: #### Benchmarks.\nL116: \nL117: KernelBench (cite97†Ouyang et al., 2025 ) provides 250 problems across three difficulty levels with standardized evaluation using torch.allclose(rtol=$10^{-4}$, atol=$10^{-4}$). We evaluate on all 250 problems plus 20 tutorial kernels.\nL118: \nL119: ## 3 Problem Formulation\nL120: ###### Definition 1 (Kernel Optimization)\nL121: Let $M_{0}=\\langle\\{k_{1},\\dots,k_{m}\\},L\\rangle$ denote a compiled model produced by torch.compile, where $\\{k_{i}\\}$ are Triton-generated sub-kernels and $L$ are extern library calls (cuBLAS, cuDNN) that remain fixed. Let $\\mathcal{K}$ be the space of syntactically valid Triton programs, $\\tau(k)$ the wall-clock sub-kernel time via do_bench, $M(k_{i}\\!\\to\\!k^{\\prime}_{i})$ the re-stitching operator that substitutes $k^{\\prime}_{i}$ into the model, and $\\mathcal{V}(k,k_{0})$ a verification function.\nL122: The optimization problem is:\nL123:  | $$k^{*}=\\arg\\min_{k\\in\\mathcal{K}}\\;\\tau(k)\\quad\\text{subject to}\\quad\\mathcal{V}(k,k_{0})=1$$  |  | (1)\nL124: \nL125: The search space $\\mathcal{K}$ is intractable to enumerate. We access it through an LLM-based transformation operator $\\mathcal{A}_{\\theta}:\\mathcal{K}\\times\\mathcal{P}\\to\\mathcal{K}$, parameterized by an LLM $\\theta$, that takes a kernel $k$ and an optimization plan $p\\in\\mathcal{P}$ (derived from NCU profiling) and produces a candidate $k^{\\prime}=\\mathcal{A}_{\\theta}(k,p)$.\nL126: ###### Definition 2 (Four-Gate Verification Cascade)\nL127: \nL128: The verification function $\\mathcal{V}(k,k_{0})$ is the conjunction of four gates applied sequentially:\nL129: \nL130:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathcal{V}(k,k_{0})$  | $\\displaystyle=\\underbrace{V_{\\text{stat}}(k)\\;\\wedge\\;V_{\\text{corr}}(k,k_{0})}_{\\text{per-candidate (Gates 1--2)}}\\;\\wedge\\;\\underbrace{V_{\\text{model}}(k,k_{0})\\;\\wedge\\;V_{\\text{perf}}(k,k_{0})}_{\\text{end-to-end (Gates 3--4)}}$  |  | (2)\nL131: $V_{\\text{stat}}$ and $V_{\\text{corr}}$ (Gates 1–2) run on every candidate during optimization; $V_{\\text{model}}$ and $V_{\\text{perf}}$ (Gates 3–4) run once on the re-stitched model afterward, with $\\gamma=1.03$ as a noise margin (full gate definitions and tolerances in §cite19†4.4 ). When $\\mathcal{V}(k^{*},k_{0})=0$ for all candidates, the system returns $k_{0}$ unchanged.\nL132: ## 4 Method\nL133: ### 4.1 System Overview\nL134: KernelOPT operates in two modes. In single-kernel mode, it optimizes a standalone Triton or Helion (cite84†Ansel and others, 2025 ) kernel directly.\nL135: In multi-kernel mode, it processes a PyTorch model through five stages: (1) Inductor-aware synthesis, where torch.compile traces the model, extern kernels (cuBLAS (cite80†NVIDIA Corporation, 2024a )/cuDNN (cite81†Chetlur et al., 2014 )) are detected and preserved, fusible groups are merged, and an LLM generates validated standalone Triton kernels; (2) baseline profiling with NVIDIA Nsight Compute (cite86†NVIDIA Corporation, 2024b ) (--set full kernel replay); (3) strategy analysis classifying the NCU bottleneck tier; (4) iterative optimization with $T$ iterations of plan generation, execution, profiling, and beam selection; and (5) re-stitch and verification, replacing each optimized sub-kernel in the full model with four-gate verification (Eq. cite98†2 ).\nL136: cite99†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: KernelOPT pipeline. A PyTorch model is traced via Inductor; sub-kernels are classified as Triton-generated (optimizable) or extern-library (preserved). Five agents iterate within a LangGraph state machine using NCU profiling feedback. Optimized kernels are re-stitched and verified at the model level.\nL137: #### Inductor-aware synthesis.\nL138: torch.compile with max_autotune produces Inductor output mixing async_compile.triton blocks and extern_kernels calls; KernelOPT detects the latter via regex and flags them needs_triton_replacement: False. Adjacent fusible Triton groups are merged and an LLM synthesizes a standalone kernel per group from its aten operation graph, validated against the eager model (allclose(rtol=atol=$10^{-4}$), 2 seeds, up to $K$ retries); only validated kernels enter the optimization loop.\nL139: This step alone can yield large gains from fusing operations Inductor kept separate (e.g., $33\\times$ on L2 018).\nL140: ### 4.2 Agent Architecture\nL141: Following AccelOpt’s planner–executor–summarizer architecture (cite77†Zhang et al., 2026 ), five agents collaborate within a LangGraph (cite85†LangChain Inc., 2024 ) state machine; we add a profiler and a strategy analyst for NCU-guided planning. The profiler configures NCU profiling (ncu --set full) from framework markers in the kernel source, then parses the CSV report into a profiling context (SOL throughput, duration, registers, occupancy, top-3 NCU rules) injected into the planner’s prompt.\nL142: The strategy analyst deterministically classifies the bottleneck tier (near-optimal, memory-bound, compute-bound, or underutilized by SOL${}_{\\text{mem}}$ vs. SOL${}_{\\text{comp}}$ against an 80% threshold), and for multi-kernel inputs makes an LLM call to decide fuse-vs-optimize grouping. The planner generates optimization plans per iteration, with a meltdown detector enforcing diversity when recent directions collapse to $\\leq 2$ unique approaches.\nL143: The executor implements each plan with up to $K$ retries, receiving compile/correctness errors as in-conversation feedback. The summarizer distills each attempt into a structured experience item.\nL144: ### 4.3 Profiling-Guided Beam Search\nL145: \nL146: Prior systems use beam search (cite77†Zhang et al., 2026 ) or repeated sampling (cite78†Wang et al., 2025 ) for candidate selection, but without profiling-guided diversity enforcement. The transformation landscape is non-convex: a locally suboptimal transformation may enable subsequent improvements unreachable by fixed-direction search.\nL147: We formulate the optimization as search over a rooted tree $G=(V,E)$, where each node $v\\in V$ represents a kernel $k_{v}\\in\\mathcal{K}$ with latency $\\tau(k_{v})$, and each edge $(u,v)$ represents a transformation $k_{v}=\\mathcal{A}_{\\theta}(k_{u},p)$. Beam selection ranks candidates by measured latency and applies DiverseSelect (Eq. cite100†3 ) to preserve one representative per active chain, balancing exploration of underexpanded branches against exploitation of the best-latency path.\n-------------------